1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,250
Pulau Ubin: The Island
Singapore Decided to Keep

2
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:07,218
Ten minutes by bumboat from Changi Point,

3
00:00:07,298 --> 00:00:09,825
Singapore becomes another country.

4
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:13,886
There are no traffic lights on
Pulau Ubin, no shopping malls,

5
00:00:13,966 --> 00:00:17,416
no housing blocks — just a
village of bicycle shops,

6
00:00:17,496 --> 00:00:23,050
kampong houses among the coconut palms,
flooded granite quarries turned into lakes,

7
00:00:23,130 --> 00:00:27,475
and a few dozen residents living
much as their grandparents did.

8
00:00:27,475 --> 00:00:31,296
Every other island around
Singapore has been remade —

9
00:00:31,376 --> 00:00:34,025
merged, built on, connected, closed.

10
00:00:34,025 --> 00:00:37,145
Ubin alone was left as it was, and in the

11
00:00:37,225 --> 00:00:40,425
end that was a decision, not an accident.

12
00:00:40,425 --> 00:00:45,100
This is the story of the island
Singapore decided to keep.

13
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:49,580
Ubin's original Malay name says
what the island is made of:

14
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:52,675
Pulau Batu Jubin, Granite Stone Island.

15
00:00:52,675 --> 00:00:57,320
Ubin itself comes from jubin, the
squared granite tiles cut from

16
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,125
its seven hills; in Hokkien the
island is Chioh-sua, Stone Hill.

17
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:05,048
What looks like one island was once five,

18
00:01:05,128 --> 00:01:10,542
separated by tidal rivers and joined
by bunds thrown up for prawn farming —

19
00:01:10,622 --> 00:01:13,984
1,020 hectares of granite,
rubber and mangrove,

20
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:17,800
the second-largest of
Singapore's offshore islands.

21
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:20,225
The stone was the point.

22
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:22,064
From the nineteenth century,

23
00:01:22,144 --> 00:01:25,775
Ubin's quarries fed the
building of Singapore itself.

24
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:30,367
Granite blocks quarried and
shaped on the island by Indian

25
00:01:30,447 --> 00:01:35,038
convict labour went into the
Horsburgh Lighthouse in 1851;

26
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:38,663
Ubin stone built the
Istana in 1869 and went,

27
00:01:38,743 --> 00:01:44,300
with Bukit Timah granite, into the
Singapore–Johore Causeway in 1923.

28
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,872
The National Parks Board
counts six quarries on

29
00:01:47,952 --> 00:01:50,980
the island — Balai, Kekek, Ketam, Pekan,

30
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:55,100
Petai and Ubin — some dug
40 metres below sea level.

31
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:58,467
The last and biggest was Ketam Quarry,

32
00:01:58,547 --> 00:02:02,175
worked by the Aik Hwa company from 1964.

33
00:02:02,175 --> 00:02:07,022
It employed more than a hundred
men, most of them islanders,

34
00:02:07,102 --> 00:02:12,443
and in the 1990s it was still
supplying 160 to 180 tons of granite

35
00:02:12,523 --> 00:02:17,450
a month — some 30 to 40 percent
of Singapore's whole demand.

36
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:20,275
When Ketam closed in May 1999,

37
00:02:20,355 --> 00:02:23,550
quarrying on Ubin ended for good.

38
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:26,493
The pits filled with
rain, and the holes the

39
00:02:26,573 --> 00:02:29,447
island's stone was
taken from became still,

40
00:02:29,527 --> 00:02:32,825
dark lakes, now among its
most beautiful sights.

41
00:02:32,825 --> 00:02:36,275
People came for the
stone, and for the land.

42
00:02:36,275 --> 00:02:40,576
The island's first inhabitants
were Orang Laut and Malays

43
00:02:40,656 --> 00:02:44,341
of Bugis and Javanese
origin; a European planter,

44
00:02:44,421 --> 00:02:48,338
Thomas Heslop Hill, opened
a coffee estate in 1879 —

45
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:52,875
coffee was so important that
the island had a Coffee Hill.

46
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:57,104
In the 1880s came the founding
migration, a move of Malay

47
00:02:57,184 --> 00:03:01,034
settlers from the Kallang
River led, it is believed,

48
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:05,650
by Encik Endut Senin (the name
is also spelled Endun Senin).

49
00:03:05,650 --> 00:03:08,591
Chinese quarry workers
followed — Hakka first,

50
00:03:08,671 --> 00:03:12,664
then Hokkien and Teochew — and
the island filled with kampongs

51
00:03:12,744 --> 00:03:15,554
named for their founders
and their features:

52
00:03:15,634 --> 00:03:19,575
Kampung Melayu, Kampung Jelutong,
Kampung Noordin, and more.

53
00:03:19,575 --> 00:03:23,575
At its height the island
held real institutions.

54
00:03:23,575 --> 00:03:29,382
Bin Kiang School, established in
1952, was the island's first school,

55
00:03:29,462 --> 00:03:34,325
and a maternity and child
health clinic followed in 1957.

56
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:37,744
The Fo Shan Teng Tua Pek
Kong Temple still keeps

57
00:03:37,824 --> 00:03:41,827
one of the last three Chinese
opera stages in Singapore,

58
00:03:41,907 --> 00:03:44,750
and its festival still runs for a week.

59
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:47,745
Islanders grew coffee, rubber, pineapple,

60
00:03:47,825 --> 00:03:51,200
coconut and durian,
farmed prawns and fished.

61
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:54,450
Then the century turned the other way.

62
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:57,665
Resettlement to the
mainland and the closing of

63
00:03:57,745 --> 00:04:01,250
the quarries emptied the
island, decade by decade.

64
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:07,732
The figures are stark: 2,028
people at the 1970 census;

65
00:04:07,812 --> 00:04:11,669
about 1,000 by 1987; 139 in 2001;

66
00:04:11,749 --> 00:04:17,475
and by 2012, just 38
residents in 24 households.

67
00:04:17,475 --> 00:04:20,629
Bin Kiang School closed in 1985

68
00:04:20,709 --> 00:04:23,525
and was demolished in 2000.

69
00:04:23,525 --> 00:04:26,767
Those who remain are known
by name — Ahmad Kassim,

70
00:04:26,847 --> 00:04:30,886
whose family's wooden house in
Kampung Melayu was built by his

71
00:04:30,966 --> 00:04:34,739
father during the war, and Ong
Ang Kui, the "Ah Ma" of the

72
00:04:34,819 --> 00:04:38,525
famous Ah Ma Drink Stall, who
sells drinks and fruit from

73
00:04:38,605 --> 00:04:42,525
the more than ninety durian
trees her late husband planted.

74
00:04:42,525 --> 00:04:46,282
On a hill path inside what
is now the Ketam Mountain

75
00:04:46,362 --> 00:04:49,677
Bike Park stands a small
shrine, and behind it

76
00:04:49,757 --> 00:04:53,225
one of Singapore's most
repeated ghost stories.

77
00:04:53,225 --> 00:04:56,880
The legend says that when
the First World War broke

78
00:04:56,960 --> 00:05:00,687
out in 1914, British soldiers
came to Ubin to intern

79
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:04,575
the German family who ran
a coffee plantation there.

80
00:05:04,575 --> 00:05:08,592
The family's daughter — eighteen
years old, in most tellings —

81
00:05:08,672 --> 00:05:13,100
fled into the jungle, fell from a
cliff or a quarry edge, and died.

82
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:17,426
Workers found her body days later,
covered in ants, and buried her

83
00:05:17,506 --> 00:05:22,032
on the hill; her spirit was kept in
an urn with a crucifix and coins,

84
00:05:22,112 --> 00:05:26,050
and gamblers began coming to
pray to her for lucky numbers.

85
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,575
It is a wonderful story.

86
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:31,445
It is also, on the evidence, not true —

87
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:34,475
and the unravelling is the better tale.

88
00:05:34,475 --> 00:05:38,954
In 2021 the historian William
Gibson searched the archives

89
00:05:39,034 --> 00:05:43,200
for the National Library
Board's BiblioAsia magazine.

90
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:48,117
The coffee estate was real, but
its German owners, the firm of D.

91
00:05:48,197 --> 00:05:53,425
Brandt and Company, had sold it in
1907, seven years before the war.

92
00:05:53,425 --> 00:05:57,624
In 1914 the estate's manager
was an Anglo-Irishman,

93
00:05:57,704 --> 00:06:02,150
and no German family appears
in the island's records.

94
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:06,067
There was no internment raid
on Ubin, and no newspaper of

95
00:06:06,147 --> 00:06:10,425
the time reports a missing girl
or parents searching for her.

96
00:06:10,425 --> 00:06:13,850
The whole story traces
back to a single informant,

97
00:06:13,930 --> 00:06:19,739
an islander born in 1928 — more than a
decade after the girl was said to have died —

98
00:06:19,819 --> 00:06:23,675
who had it from another man
who had not seen it either.

99
00:06:23,675 --> 00:06:28,175
The shrine itself is genuine,
and older than the legend.

100
00:06:28,175 --> 00:06:32,497
It began as a datuk keramat, a
local spirit shrine, on a granite

101
00:06:32,577 --> 00:06:36,967
hill that has since been quarried
away entirely — the spot is now

102
00:06:37,047 --> 00:06:41,506
the Ketam Quarry pond — and one
of its traditions says the "grave"

103
00:06:41,586 --> 00:06:46,125
was a termite mound, which across
the region often become shrines.

104
00:06:46,125 --> 00:06:49,407
When the quarrying came
close, it was the Aik Hwa

105
00:06:49,487 --> 00:06:52,633
quarry company itself
that moved the shrine and

106
00:06:52,713 --> 00:06:56,075
built it a yellow hut by
the sea, out of respect.

107
00:06:56,075 --> 00:07:00,319
The shrine was rebuilt in
brick in 2015, with a statue

108
00:07:00,399 --> 00:07:06,164
of the girl holding coffee berries
and a sign reading "Berlin Heiligtum",

109
00:07:06,244 --> 00:07:09,767
dated 1896 — a year that
is itself a mistake,

110
00:07:09,847 --> 00:07:13,050
since the First World War began in 1914.

111
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:16,020
Worshippers still come, and the offerings

112
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:19,150
now include Barbie dolls and nail polish.

113
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:22,547
The island kept the story,
and kept the shrine,

114
00:07:22,627 --> 00:07:26,325
long after the facts had
quietly left both behind.

115
00:07:26,325 --> 00:07:30,275
Ubin's war, when it came,
was brief and strange.

116
00:07:30,275 --> 00:07:34,377
On the evening of the 7th of
February 1942 the Japanese

117
00:07:34,457 --> 00:07:40,004
occupied the island, and the next day
their guns on Ubin bombarded Changi,

118
00:07:40,084 --> 00:07:43,050
drawing the fortress artillery's reply.

119
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:45,225
It was a diversion.

120
00:07:45,225 --> 00:07:48,577
That night, while Singapore's
defences looked east,

121
00:07:48,657 --> 00:07:52,425
the real invasion crossed the
Johore Strait in the west.

122
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:55,158
Twenty-five years later the island

123
00:07:55,238 --> 00:07:58,050
found its most famous institution.

124
00:07:58,050 --> 00:08:03,420
In 1967 the People's Association
established the Outward Bound

125
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:08,950
School of Singapore on Pulau
Ubin, set up by Dr Goh Keng Swee.

126
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:12,891
The British Army had run a holiday
adventure camp on the island

127
00:08:12,971 --> 00:08:17,614
since the late 1950s, and British
officers ran the school's first courses;

128
00:08:17,694 --> 00:08:21,890
the first intake included a
fifteen-year-old named Lee Hsien Loong,

129
00:08:21,970 --> 00:08:25,529
who remembered, fifty years
on, an island of prawn ponds,

130
00:08:25,609 --> 00:08:29,358
granite quarries, a few kampongs
and old rubber plantations,

131
00:08:29,438 --> 00:08:34,225
and training built on canoes, sailing
dinghies, compasses and rope courses.

132
00:08:34,225 --> 00:08:38,145
The Defence Ministry ran the
school from 1970 to 1991,

133
00:08:38,225 --> 00:08:42,960
toughening teenage boys before
National Service; it then returned

134
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:47,034
to the People's Association
as Outward Bound Singapore,

135
00:08:47,114 --> 00:08:51,924
and its new campus — which it
describes as the first purpose-built

136
00:08:52,004 --> 00:08:56,300
modern Outward Bound centre
in the world — opened in 1997.

137
00:08:56,300 --> 00:09:00,120
Today, under a national
outdoor education masterplan,

138
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:04,683
every Secondary 3 student in
Singapore goes through a five-day

139
00:09:04,763 --> 00:09:09,325
Outward Bound expedition programme,
and Ubin remains its home.

140
00:09:09,325 --> 00:09:12,011
I know this part of the island's story

141
00:09:12,091 --> 00:09:15,075
at first hand, because I saw it as a boy.

142
00:09:15,075 --> 00:09:19,297
A friend of mine — from home, from
Holy Innocents Lane in Aukang,

143
00:09:19,377 --> 00:09:22,606
where our families' roots
ran deep — had become an

144
00:09:22,686 --> 00:09:26,525
instructor at the school, and
I visited him on the island.

145
00:09:26,525 --> 00:09:29,969
To a boy it seemed an
adventurer's life: the camp,

146
00:09:30,049 --> 00:09:33,925
the boats, the jungle, the
whole island as a workplace.

147
00:09:33,925 --> 00:09:37,288
I slept in the trainees'
bunk one night and was woken

148
00:09:37,368 --> 00:09:40,860
by loud bangs going off around
us; my friend explained,

149
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:44,367
quite matter-of-factly, that
they were thunderflashes,

150
00:09:44,447 --> 00:09:47,825
which the instructors set
off around their trainees.

151
00:09:47,825 --> 00:09:52,152
He talked, too, about the school's
tradition of the solo night —

152
00:09:52,232 --> 00:09:55,950
a trainee sent out alone
overnight, a rite of passing.

153
00:09:55,950 --> 00:10:00,150
Thousands of Singaporean boys
passed through that world.

154
00:10:00,150 --> 00:10:03,775
For most of them, Ubin was not a day trip.

155
00:10:03,775 --> 00:10:06,950
It was where they were
sent to be toughened.

156
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:11,260
At the island's eastern tip lies
Chek Jawa, a hundred hectares

157
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:14,871
where six habitats meet —
sandy beach, rocky shore,

158
00:10:14,951 --> 00:10:19,200
seagrass lagoon, coral rubble,
mangroves and coastal forest.

159
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:23,936
It was slated for reclamation, and
was saved at the very end of 2001,

160
00:10:24,016 --> 00:10:28,193
days before the work was due to
begin, when a survey revealed

161
00:10:28,273 --> 00:10:32,600
how much life the shore held and
the public took up its cause.

162
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:36,311
That story has its own post
on this blog — Chek Jawa:

163
00:10:36,391 --> 00:10:41,031
The Shore That Was Saved by a
Survey — and since 2007 a boardwalk,

164
00:10:41,111 --> 00:10:45,250
a viewing tower and a visitor
centre in a 1930s Tudor-style

165
00:10:45,330 --> 00:10:49,550
house have let visitors see
the shore without trampling it.

166
00:10:49,550 --> 00:10:53,835
For all its nearness to one of
the most wired cities on earth,

167
00:10:53,915 --> 00:10:58,350
Pulau Ubin has never been connected
to the national power grid.

168
00:10:58,350 --> 00:11:02,933
For generations its people drew
water from wells and ran their

169
00:11:03,013 --> 00:11:07,600
lights and fans off diesel
generators — noisy, and expensive.

170
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:12,409
As recently as 2025, the Government
told Parliament that the island

171
00:11:12,489 --> 00:11:17,079
has around sixty residential and
business electricity consumers,

172
00:11:17,159 --> 00:11:21,975
and that connecting it to the
mainland grid is not cost-effective.

173
00:11:21,975 --> 00:11:25,450
So Ubin became an experiment instead.

174
00:11:25,450 --> 00:11:29,919
On the 10th of October 2013
the Energy Market Authority

175
00:11:29,999 --> 00:11:34,715
inaugurated the Pulau Ubin
Micro-Grid Test-Bed, built by a

176
00:11:34,795 --> 00:11:39,675
consortium of Daily Life
Renewable Energy and OKH Holdings.

177
00:11:39,675 --> 00:11:43,879
Around thirty homes and
businesses near the jetty were

178
00:11:43,959 --> 00:11:48,322
connected to a small grid of
their own, powered by solar

179
00:11:48,402 --> 00:11:52,368
panels and biodiesel
generators, at 80 cents a unit

180
00:11:52,448 --> 00:11:57,050
against about $1.20 for
home-generated diesel electricity.

181
00:11:57,050 --> 00:12:00,141
Newly able to run chillers
and air-conditioners,

182
00:12:00,221 --> 00:12:03,325
the village shops changed
what they could sell.

183
00:12:03,325 --> 00:12:07,864
In 2023 the system was
upgraded by EDP Renewables,

184
00:12:07,944 --> 00:12:14,225
with 328 kilowatts of solar panels
and a megawatt-hour flow battery.

185
00:12:14,225 --> 00:12:18,200
The honest coda is in
the parliamentary record.

186
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:22,576
In a written reply in March 2026,
the Government reported that

187
00:12:22,656 --> 00:12:27,175
in 2025 solar supplied only about
10 percent of the micro-grid's

188
00:12:27,255 --> 00:12:30,624
electricity and diesel
the remaining 90 percent,

189
00:12:30,704 --> 00:12:35,511
and that there were thirteen power
trips that year — a long way from

190
00:12:35,591 --> 00:12:39,975
the operator's publicity about
an island running on sunshine.

191
00:12:39,975 --> 00:12:43,838
A better battery is due later in 2026,

192
00:12:43,918 --> 00:12:47,550
and the tariff is fixed until 2034.

193
00:12:47,550 --> 00:12:51,042
The experiment continues,
which is perhaps the right

194
00:12:51,122 --> 00:12:54,900
way to see it: Ubin was never
the grid's success story.

195
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:58,865
It is the place Singapore tries
things out, at village scale,

196
00:12:58,945 --> 00:13:02,725
where the results are measured
in one community's lights.

197
00:13:02,725 --> 00:13:07,306
In 2016 the National Parks
Board became the island's single

198
00:13:07,386 --> 00:13:12,125
managing agency — it had
previously been split among twelve.

199
00:13:12,125 --> 00:13:16,474
The Ubin Project, launched in
2014 with the island's community,

200
00:13:16,554 --> 00:13:20,552
carries a promise worth quoting
exactly: "We are committed

201
00:13:20,632 --> 00:13:24,850
to retain Ubin in its rustic
state for as long as possible."

202
00:13:24,850 --> 00:13:29,476
Its next phase, launched at the
project's tenth anniversary,

203
00:13:29,556 --> 00:13:32,613
runs to 2034 and includes a programme to

204
00:13:32,693 --> 00:13:36,850
restore kampong houses and
an Ubin School initiative.

205
00:13:36,850 --> 00:13:41,950
About 300,000 visitors a
year make the crossing now.

206
00:13:41,950 --> 00:13:45,189
The bumboats leave Changi
Point Ferry Terminal

207
00:13:45,269 --> 00:13:48,363
when about a dozen
passengers have gathered,

208
00:13:48,443 --> 00:13:52,050
charge $4 a person each
way, and take ten minutes.

209
00:13:52,050 --> 00:13:55,150
On the island, the bicycle
shops by the jetty —

210
00:13:55,230 --> 00:13:58,940
the village's main economy —
hire out bikes for the day,

211
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:03,609
and the roads lead past the quarries
to the Ketam Mountain Bike Park,

212
00:14:03,689 --> 00:14:07,275
Singapore's first, built
around the old Ketam Quarry.

213
00:14:07,275 --> 00:14:11,305
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore's other islands

214
00:14:11,385 --> 00:14:15,700
were remade for industry, housing
or leisure, each in its turn.

215
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:18,976
Ubin's distinction is that
the remaking stopped —

216
00:14:19,056 --> 00:14:23,222
first because the quarries closed
and the people drifted away,

217
00:14:23,302 --> 00:14:27,810
and then, deliberately, because
the country decided that one island

218
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:31,725
should stay as a reminder of
what all of them once were.

219
00:14:31,725 --> 00:14:35,432
The last villagers, the
schoolboys on their solo nights,

220
00:14:35,512 --> 00:14:38,476
and a grid that runs an
experiment at village

221
00:14:38,556 --> 00:14:41,993
scale are all part of the
same decision: to keep one

222
00:14:42,073 --> 00:14:45,725
place where Singapore can
still see its own beginning.
